<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bahri Mamluks</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bahri Mamluk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bahri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> were a regiment of slave soldiers formed by the Ayyubid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/as-salih-ayyub">al-Salih Ayyub</a> and stationed on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, from whose name ('bahr', river) they took their title. They overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1250 and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260. Their leaders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> until 1382.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive consolidation came in 1260, when Sultan Qutuz and the <b>Bahri Mamluk</b> commander Baybars defeated the Mongols at Ain Jalut; Baybars then seized the throne (r. 1260–1277) and established the sultanate's military credibility by campaigning against both Mongols and Crusader states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Kiswe, south of Damascus, the <b>Bahri Mamluks</b> of Egypt met the Mongols of the Ilkhanate and their Armenian allies in a battle lasting until 22 April.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Barquq, a Circassian who had come to Egypt as a slave, deposed Sultan al-Salih Hajji, the last of the <b>Bahri Mamluks</b>, who had ruled Egypt since 1250.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near the spring of Ain Jalut in the Jezreel Valley, the <b>Bahri Mamluks</b> of Egypt under Qutuz and Baybars defeated a Mongol army of the Ilkhanate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-3">3 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
